00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:14.160
Deep, in the mountains of Austria, lies the
small, but scenic town of Hallstatt.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:21.740
But this isn’t that, It’s an exact replica,
built 9,000 kilometers away, near Hong Kong.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:22.740
Austria.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:23.740
China.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:29.599
It’s home to European architecture, Chinese
cuisine, and all the traffic of…

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:30.990
North Korea.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:36.149
Because, during the day, it may be the wedding
photo capital of the region,

00:00:36.149 --> 00:00:40.569
But after the sun sets, its cottages become
remarkably quiet.

00:00:40.569 --> 00:00:47.799
China’s lookalike towns, of places like
Paris, Berlin, London, and Jackson Hole, Wyoming,

00:00:47.799 --> 00:00:48.979
aren’t alone.

00:00:48.979 --> 00:00:54.530
In many places, across China, there are far
more houses than there are people.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:01:00.690
Long rows of apartments, even entire cities,
sit completely, or mostly empty.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:08.070
In total, approximately 50 million units,
Or 22% of China’s entire urban housing.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.530
But this doesn’t mean they aren’t being
bought.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.340
Because, they are.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:13.540
Like crazy.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:18.670
Ten years ago, most people were, as you’d
expect, buying homes for the first time.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.130
Today, it looks like this.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:27.390
Second homes are the majority, and people
are buying almost as many third homes as first!

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.259
These aren’t cheap, either.

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:34.290
In Los Angeles, the price per square foot
is $633.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.479
In Shenzhen, 805.

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:41.920
And, close your eyes, because you don’t
even wanna know the price in Hong Kong.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.369
Now consider the difference in wages.

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:51.749
The average annual income in Shenzhen is around
7,500 US Dollars, compared to 60 thousand

00:01:51.749 --> 00:01:53.159
in LA.

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:55.649
Something clearly doesn’t add up.

00:01:55.649 --> 00:02:01.670
People in China are buying homes like Americans
buy… cars, But they’re leaving them empty,

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:06.619
And it’s not clear where the money is coming
from, or why they’re being built.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:11.900
The usual explanation is that China’s government
is so desperate for economic growth that it

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.620
builds bridges to nowhere and houses to…
look at.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:20.090
But that’s only one part of a much bigger
story.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:24.239
China’s troubles begin with its political
system.

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:28.069
The Central Government is the highest level
of its only party.

00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:32.090
Here, laws are written and the fate of the
nation, decided.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.989
Beijing is THE ultimate authority.

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:41.090
It appoints everyone from secretaries to governors,
and isn’t afraid to move them around should

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:44.049
any one official gain too much influence.

00:02:44.049 --> 00:02:49.680
BUT - it would also be a mistake to see China
as one, singular power.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:56.180
Because below the central government is a
network of local divisions: 22 regional provinces,

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:02.150
4 municipalities, 4 autonomous regions, and
2 Special Administrative.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.680
Under those are over 300 prefectures.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.569
Followed by the less important counties, townships,
and villages.

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:16.590
Now, just as Californians have different concerns
than do Texans or Floridians,

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:22.980
China is a big country, and the interests
of a coastal exporter like Shenzhen are very

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:28.599
different than those of, say, a more independent
region like Inner Mongolia.

00:03:28.599 --> 00:03:31.989
The same is true for different levels of government.

00:03:31.989 --> 00:03:36.989
While Beijing writes the rules, cities apply
and enforce them.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:39.110
Often, very differently.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:45.451
And there’s one, awkward little detail:
Cities receive just 40% of tax revenue, but

00:03:45.451 --> 00:03:49.200
are responsible for 80% of their expenses.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.159
So, naturally, they need another source of
income.

00:03:53.159 --> 00:03:56.250
And this is where things get interesting.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.819
In China, rural land is collectively owned.

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:05.420
Everyone, and also no-one, owns it, which
means it can’t be the location of a new

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.120
luxury apartment.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.551
But luckily for cities, they have the power
to rezone land from rural to urban, which

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:14.099
can be developed.

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:18.090
In other words, they own a money printing
machine.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:25.220
Watch this: First, a city buys cheap, rural
land, Which it then redefines as urban, And

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:29.720
finally, sells to developers at its now, much
higher, price.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:30.720
Like.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:31.720
Magic.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.630
Over, and over, and over, again.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:39.930
Cities get much-needed cash, and developers
build housing like it’s nobody’s business.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:45.970
Now, unlike states in America, local governments
here are generally forbidden from taking loans.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:49.260
But, again, there’s a loophole.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:55.010
Cities can create a Local Government Financial
Vehicle, which is a fancy way of saying, a

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:56.110
state-owned company.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:01.630
And by “giving” it that new urban land,
the “company” can do what the city legally

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.550
can’t: borrow money.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:10.880
Which, they can use to build roads, schools,
and, on occasion, replica Austrian towns.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:16.750
This is so effective that, in some years,
land sales account for 40% of local government

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:17.750
revenue.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:24.080
Plus, all this construction increases GDP,
which just so happens to be the way officials

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.120
get promoted.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.420
It’s a perfect system.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:29.550
At least, until it’s not.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:34.220
If, or, when, housing prices fall, so does
city revenue.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.770
And, all those loans probably won’t magically
disappear.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:44.770
Beijing wants to avoid a housing crisis, but
cities just want to survive, and governors,

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:48.090
get promoted, which puts the two at odds.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:53.720
Eventually, cities start running out of land
to sell, and have no choice but to build more.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:58.460
Like this one, which spent 2 billion dollars
blowing up the tops of mountains.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:03.430
Those developers who purchase that land, by
the way, are required to use it, which leads

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:08.000
to many, often quickly-constructed, low-quality,
houses.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.830
And that brings us to the second question:
why are people buying them?

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.580
And doing it like their life depended on it?

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:18.810
Well, for one, because it kinda does.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:25.260
Thanks to the famous One Child Policy, China
now has the entire population of Canada more

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:26.970
men than women.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:30.410
And that means fierce competition for marriage.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:35.810
Men are expected to own at least one property
before even being considered.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.530
It’s one of the most important elements
of social status.

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:45.940
For many, real estate isn’t just an opportunity,
it’s a downright social necessity.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:51.160
Because of this, friends and family pool money
together to help buy homes for their children.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:57.370
And that’s how, nearly everyone, in a country
with the per capita GDP of the Dominican Republic,

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:01.410
can afford some of the most expensive homes
on the planet.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.080
The other big factor is that Chinese citizens
Save.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.080
Like.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:07.080
Crazy.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:11.880
When it comes to saving money, there’s China,
and then there’s basically everyone else.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:17.940
Where, Europeans put 4 percent of their disposable
income in the piggy bank, Chinese drop nearly

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.100
40!

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.460
The problem is, where can they put it?.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:28.250
China’s domestic stock market is just too
risky, And its banks are often seen as unpredictable.

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:32.220
Which makes real estate a Chinese investor’s
best friend.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:36.500
It alone accounts for 70% of all household
wealth.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:41.850
It also doesn’t hurt that property tax is
a beautiful 0%.

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:47.720
When taxes are only paid upfront, why wouldn’t
you buy as soon as possible, and just sit

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:48.720
on it?

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:53.190
Put all this together, and you have a recipe
for extreme house buying.

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:57.840
An amazing 90% of homes are owned by their
residents.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.680
Europe and the U.S., stand at 69 and 64%,
respectively.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.530
And while we’re on the subject of crazy
high numbers, Ninety-four percent of Chinese

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:12.670
millennials who don’t already own, plan
on buying in the next five years.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:16.490
What else do 94% of people agree on?

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:20.090
Not even China can quench this thirst for
real estate.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:25.770
Despite laws against it, billions of dollars
flow out of the country every year into foreign

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:26.770
property.

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:32.699
It’s so common in places like Vancouver,
that, earlier this year, it introduced a 20%

00:08:32.699 --> 00:08:34.019
tax for foreigners.

00:08:34.019 --> 00:08:38.970
The irony is that while cities like Beijing
and Hong Kong have so little room, people

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:44.079
are forced to sleep underground, these 50
million homes can’t find renters.

00:08:44.079 --> 00:08:49.759
So, hey, if you live in California, I think
I may have found an escape plan.

00:08:49.759 --> 00:08:55.339
Anyway, not only are these homes bought without
interiors, literally just concrete walls,

00:08:55.339 --> 00:09:00.139
but they’re also usually located outside
city centers, where there aren’t as many

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:01.139
jobs.

00:09:01.139 --> 00:09:07.029
Now, the assumption in all of this, is that,
eventually, people will come, And speculation

00:09:07.029 --> 00:09:08.890
will become reality.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:13.529
The Eastern side of Shanghai, for example,
was once laughed at by Milton Friedman for

00:09:13.529 --> 00:09:15.089
being totally empty.

00:09:15.089 --> 00:09:21.730
Today, as a financial capital of the world,
with a GDP of 400 billion, we can pretty safely

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:24.709
say it’s proven the haters wrong.

00:09:24.709 --> 00:09:31.250
China is in the process of migrating 300 million
people from country to city, And, of course,

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:33.530
they’ll need a place to live.

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:37.060
Inevitably, many of these cities will spring
to life.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:39.220
That doesn’t mean everything is peachy.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:42.870
A few things are decidedly not peachy.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:48.880
First, remember that the vast majority of
empty homes is expensive, commodity housing.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.699
These are not the kinds of places you buy
coming from a farm in the country.

00:09:53.699 --> 00:09:57.320
And second, all these homes have an expiration
date.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:03.020
In China, a building can be owned, but the
land beneath it can only ever be leased - from

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.259
the government, for 70 years.

00:10:05.259 --> 00:10:09.310
After that, it’s anyone’s guess whether
ownership will be renewed.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:11.130
And if so, for how much.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:15.670
But, the truth is, 70 years is pretty optimistic…

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:20.779
Think about it this way: If construction is
good for GDP, why build once, when you can

00:10:20.779 --> 00:10:23.810
build and re-build every few years?

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:28.980
It’s kinda like the iPhone, if you’d like
to upgrade every year, Apple will happily

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:30.550
sell you a new phone.

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:32.339
It’s certainly not judging.

00:10:32.339 --> 00:10:37.160
Except, in the case of China’s housing,
developers are incentivized to make short-term

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:43.009
bets, they know their homes will only last
a few decades anyway, which means using lower

00:10:43.009 --> 00:10:44.490
quality materials.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:51.569
Meanwhile, cities continue taking loans and
housing prices continue rising unsustainably.

00:10:51.569 --> 00:10:53.839
Of course, Beijing knows all this.

00:10:53.839 --> 00:10:59.430
It’s aware of the bubble, the risks involved,
and it knows more or less how to fix it - some

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:04.839
combination of slowing down lending, reining-in
local governments, and introducing a property

00:11:04.839 --> 00:11:06.870
tax, like Shanghai.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:12.410
The problem is, real estate is so intertwined
with its GDP, that any of these solutions

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:15.940
would seriously risk slowing down its economy.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:21.439
In the coming decades, the world will watch
as China does its best to carefully balance

00:11:21.439 --> 00:11:27.200
its enormous challenges with its relentless
desire to grow its economy and realize The

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:29.060
Chinese Dream.

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:34.440
As Beijing prepares for economic change by
diversifying its revenue, You and I should

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:35.529
do the same.

